  "title": "Graphs and Equations",
  "body": " Graphs and Equations   This Lesson emphasizes the relationship between a graph and an equation or inequality. This is a difficult concept for students, and it is worth pointing out to the class that understanding this connection is the goal of the Lesson. Stress that they must show their work on the graph. They will want to solve all the problems algebraically, but you could mention that they will soon encounter equations that they cannot solve with algebra.  Try to make students understand that, for a linear inequality, all the solutions lie on one side of the boundary value. This is why using a single test value works. The technique will have to be modified slightly when we solve nonlinear inequalities.  While they work on the Activities, walk around to check each student's graphs, to see if they are getting it. If one person in the group is marking the graphs correctly, ask him or her to demonstrate the graphical technique to the others in the group.   Evaluating and Solving  Use the graph of to complete the tasks.    Evaluate when .   Solve when .   Find the answers to parts (a) and (b) algebraically, and compare with the answers you obtained from the graph.    Solving Equations Graphically  Use the graph of to solve the equations. (Show your work by making a dot at the correct point on the graph for each equation.)    Solve .    Solution:  Solve .    Solution:  Solve .    Solution:   d. Look back at your work in Activity 2. Use the graph to show that the following three statements are different ways of saying the same thing:  The point lies on the graph of .   is a solution of the equation .   is a solution of the equation .    Solving Inequalities Graphically   Solve .  Point out that is the same as , so we are looking for -values that produce points on the graph with -coordinate less than 7.          Instead of using test points, students find it easier if you ask which half of the line shows points with . Shade that half of the line, then ask which half of the -axis produces that portion of the line. (You can demonstrate with specific -values.)      Solve .              Use the graphs of to solve the inequalities:  Ask students to do these two problems on their own, showing their work on the graphs.     Solution:    Solution:   Graph each solution on a number line:  How does the graph on the number line compare to your solution on the -axis in each problem?    Check Your Understanding   What are the two skills compared in Activity 1? What is the difference in the methods for performing the two skills?  What was different about the problems in Activity 3, compared to those in Activity 2?  How were the solutions you found in Activity 3 different from the solutions in Activity 2?  In Activity 3, how do you decide which side of the boundary point to shade?   Wrap-Up  In this Lesson, we worked on the following skills related to linear models:   Recognize a solution of an equation or an inequality  Solve a linear inequality, and graph the solutions on a number line  Recognize a solution of an equation in two variables  Use the connection between the graph of an equation and its solutions  Use a graph to solve an equation or inequality in one variable   Questions for Writing or Discussion   In Activity 2 we used a graph of to solve the equation . Why can we do this? (How are the two equations related?)  In Activity 3a we shaded one side of the boundary value, , on the -axis, and said that the solutions of the inequality are any -values with . Verify the solution by checking two -values less than 3, and two -values greater than 3.  Suppose you have an increasing graph, for example, . If you know that for a particular -value, say, , how do you know that for all ?  In Activity 2 we solved the equation by using the graph of . Explain why the following statement is incorrect: \"The solution of is .\"   Concept Questions   Suppose we use the graph of to solve the equation . What do we call the point on the graph that supplies the solution of this equation?   The -intercept  The -intercept  The intersection point  The solution point   Is a solution of ? Is a solution of ?   Yes, Yes  No, No  Yes, No  No, Yes   A classmate says that any inequality of the form has solutions of the form , for some number . Under what conditions is this statement false?   is negative  is negative  is negative  All of the above   If is the solution for , what is the solution for ?      Not enough information to tell    "
